  <ferc:DisclosureOfImportantDisclosuresRegardingTheFinancialStatementsTextBlock id="f-61" contextRef="c-1">&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Introduction&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The accompanying financial statements were prepared in accordance with the accounting requirements of the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission ("FERC") as set forth in its applicable Uniform System of Accounts and published accounting releases, which is a comprehensive basis of accounting that varies from generally accepted accounting principles in the United States of America ("GAAP"). These requirements differ from GAAP related to (1) the presentation of deferred income taxes, (2) the presentation of regulatory debits and credits, (3) the presentation of regulatory assets and liabilities, (4) treatment of equity carrying charges, (5) amortization of goodwill, (6) the presentation of accruals associated with the cost of removal included within the accumulated depreciation reserve, and (7) the presentation of the provision of rate refund and accrued interest. Accordingly, certain footnotes are not prepared on the same basis as the financial statements presented herein.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The accompanying financial statements should be read in conjunction with the 2025 FERC Form 1 of NextEra Energy Transmission MidAtlantic, Inc. (the "Company"). In the opinion of the Company's management, all adjustments considered necessary for fair financial statement presentation have been made. The results of operations for an interim period generally will not give a true indication of results for the year. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:center"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;NEXTERA ENERGY TRANSMISSION MIDATLANTIC, INC. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:center"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;NOTES TO FINANCIAL STATEMENTS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:center"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;FOR THE NINE MONTHS ENDED SEPTEMBER 30, 2026 and 2025&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:center"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;(in thousands, unless otherwise noted)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;1. &#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;REGULATORY MATTERS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Formula Rate Filings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt;On November 22, 2023, the Company filed a request with FERC for certain transmission rate incentives with respect to its competitively-awarded Mid-Atlantic Resiliency Link transmission project. The incentives requested included authorization to recover 100% of abandoned plant costs if the project is canceled for reasons outside of the Company's control and authorization to include 100% of prudently incurred Construction Work In Progress in rate base for the project. FERC granted the request on January 19, 2024. Since CWIP in rate base during the construction period is considered an acceleration of a return on the allowance for funds used during construction, the Company believes that prior to the Project going into service, CWIP in rate base is recorded as an Alternative Revenue Program (&#8220;ARP&#8221;), with any over or under recovery reflected as a regulatory asset or liability. The income statement offset is reflected within revenue from regulatory mechanisms. Through September 30, 2026, the Company has recognized $8 million related to this program.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On March 13, 2024, the Company filed a Notice of Succession to notify the FERC that it changed its name from NextEra Energy Transmission MidAtlantic Indiana, Inc. to NextEra Energy Transmission MidAtlantic, Inc.. The filing also made changes to the Company&#8217;s formula rate template and implementation protocols to reflect its new name. The FERC accepted the Company&#8217;s Notice of Succession and revised tariff records in a delegated letter order issued April 12, 2024.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On March 27, 2024, the Company filed an application for authorization under section 204 of the Federal Power Act to request authorization to make available long-term, secured debt securities in a principal amount not to exceed $250,000,000 in order to finance ongoing capital needs associated with construction of the Mid-Atlantic Resiliency Link transmission project, in addition to other construction upgrades, and the ownership and operation of the transmission assets. The FERC accepted the Company&#8217;s request in an order issued May 10, 2024. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On June 12, 2024, the Company filed proposed revisions to increase the base return on equity (ROE) in its formula rate, Attachment H-33B to the PJM Tariff, from 9.6% to 11.0%. On May 28, 2025, the Company filed a Settlement Agreement providing for a base ROE of 10.27% or 10.77% when combined with its previously authorized RTO Participation Adder. The Settlement also included provisions addressing NEET MA&#8217;s capital structure and the accrual rate that will apply to the extent that NEET MA accrues AFUDC on a construction &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;project. On September 3, 2025, FERC approved the Settlement. NEET MA filed a compliance filing to implement the settlement ROE on October 3, 2025. FERC accepted the compliance filing on December 17, 2025 (ER24-2255).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On July 22, 2025 NEET MA filed edits to its formula rate in Attachment H-33 and H-33A to the PJM Tariff to remove legacy language that implied that the entirety of NEET MA's ATRR should be allocated to the ComEd zone. NEET MA submitted an amendment to its filing on September 12, 2025. The purpose of the edits is to eliminate any conflict between Attachment H-33 and the cost allocation provisions in Schedule 12 Appendix A, section 32 of the PJM Tariff. FERC accepted the compliance filing on November 7, 2025 (ER25-2941).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On March 6, 2026, as supplemented on April 10, 2026, NEET MidAtlantic filed for authorization under section 204 of the Federal Power Act to request authorization to make available long-term, secured debt securities in a principal amount not to exceed $300 million during a two year period. The proceeds will be used to fund the ongoing capital needs associated with the construction of the MidAtlantic Resiliency Link transmission project as well as capital upgrades, as well as working capital needs associated with NEET MidAtlantic's existing assets. FERC authorized the financing in an order issued May 1, 2026 (ES26-31). &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On July 2, 2026, the Maryland Energy Administration, Maryland Office of People's Counsel, and the Maryland Public Service Commission ("Maryland State Agencies") filed a complaint against NEET MA, among others, alleging that the 50-basis point RTO Participation Adder included in NEET MA's ROE is unjust and unreasonable. NEET MidAtlantic filed a motion to dismiss the complaint on August 5, 2026. The proceeding remains pending before FERC (EL26-87).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;State Certificate of Public Convenience and Necessity (CPCN) Filings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On January 30, 2026, NEET MA filed applications for CPCNs with the Maryland Public Service Commission (MPSC) in Case No. 9857 and West Virginia Public Service Commission (WV PSC) in Case No. 26-0075-E-CN. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On March 3, 2026, NEET MA filed applications with the Pennsylvania Public Utilities Commission (PAPUC) for certificate of public convenience (CPC) and siting approval in Docket Nos. A-2026-3060921 and A-2026-3060856. On March 5, 2026, NEET VA filed a CPCN application with the Virginia State Corporation Commission (VSCC) in Docket No. PUR-2026-00018. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;In West Virginia, an evidentiary hearing is scheduled in late October/ early November 2026. In Virginia, an evidentiary hearing is scheduled in December 2026. In Pennsylvania, an evidentiary hearing on NEET MA's CPC application is scheduled in February 2027. In Maryland, evidentiary hearings are expected in fall 2027. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;2. &#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;ELECTRIC UTILITY PLANT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Electric utility plant consists of the following:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;table style="border-collapse:collapse;display:inline-table;margin-bottom:5pt;vertical-align:text-bottom;width:100.000%"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:58.959%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:18.574%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.391%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:18.576%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:1.5pt 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;September 30, 2026&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:1.5pt 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;December 31, 2025&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:1.5pt 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Land Rights&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:1.5pt 0 1.5pt 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:1.5pt 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;506&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:1.5pt 1pt 1.5pt 